(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-pharm-exam-2-master-exam-form-d"]={"title":"Pharmacology I Exam 2 Master Exam — Form D","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which recent event contraindicates fibrinolytics?","choices":["A recent blood draw","Recent major surgery","A recent dental cleaning","A recent vaccination"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Recent surgery (including organ biopsy, puncture of noncompressible vessels, serious head or facial trauma and cardiopulmonary resuscitation) contraindicates fibrinolytics because of bleeding. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A patient has an intraocular pressure of 26 mmHg with a normal optic nerve and no visual field loss. What is this called?","choices":["Open angle glaucoma","Angle closure glaucoma","Normal tension glaucoma","Ocular hypertension"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pressure above the normal 10 to 21 mmHg but with no optic nerve damage and no field loss. It is a risk factor rather than a disease, and whether to treat depends on the other risk factors. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Which adverse effect profile is typical of bile acid sequestrants?","choices":["Flushing, itching, raised uric acid","Myalgia, headache, raised enzymes","Bloating, flatulence, constipation","Gallstones, nausea, myopathy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Resins cause gastrointestinal effects -- bloating, flatulence, fullness, constipation and nausea -- plus malabsorption of fat-soluble vitamins and folic acid. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which features characterize Reye's syndrome?","choices":["Fever, a rash and migratory joint pain","Vomiting, neurological damage, liver injury and low glucose","Jaundice with painless distension of the abdomen","Isolated hepatic failure without neurological signs"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The hypoglycemia is easy to forget alongside the liver and brain findings, and it is treatable. The neurological damage is progressive, and the glucose is genuinely low rather than borderline. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A patient treated for hypertension asks about pseudoephedrine for a cold. What is the concern?","choices":["It reduces the effect of antihypertensive medicines","It increases the effect of antihypertensives, causing hypotension","It has no effect on blood pressure treatment","It cannot be taken with any antibiotic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A patient can undo their blood pressure treatment with something bought off the shelf, without ever mentioning it as a medicine. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A patient with acute coronary syndrome has severe wheezing from reactive airway disease. Which drug class calls for caution?","choices":["Nitrates","Heparins","Fibrinolytics","Beta blockers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Severe reactive airway disease is a listed caution for beta blockers in acute coronary syndrome. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which calcium channel type do these drugs block in vascular smooth muscle, cardiac myocytes and nodal tissue?","choices":["L-type channels","T-type channels","Ryanodine receptors","IP3 receptors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. L-type channels sit in vascular smooth muscle, cardiac myocytes and the sinoatrial and atrioventricular nodes, and all three drug groups bind them. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What adverse effects are described with guaifenesin?","choices":["Confusion and agitation","Nausea and vomiting","Hypertension and tachycardia","Hepatic failure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A short list, which is part of why it is available without prescription. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 51-year-old is prescribed a topical steroid for anterior uveitis. How long may the course run?","choices":["Less than two weeks, as a pulse","Less than 72 hours","Up to three months","As long as inflammation persists"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The risks — cataract, raised pressure, infection, delayed healing, corneal ulcers — are why. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What should a patient starting benazepril be told about potassium?","choices":["Take a daily potassium supplement to replace losses","Avoid potassium supplements and salt substitutes","Potassium is not affected by this medicine","Expect low potassium, so watch for cramps"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitors raise potassium, and supplements or potassium-based salt substitutes add to it; renal disease raises the risk further. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"During a nitroprusside infusion, a patient develops trembling, vomiting and convulsions. Sodium thiosulfate is given. Which toxicity does it limit?","choices":["Thiocyanate toxicity","Lupus syndrome","Hyperkalemia","Cyanide toxicity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Each nitroprusside molecule carries five cyanide groups; cyanide toxicity causes trembling, vomiting and convulsions, and sodium thiosulfate limits it. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"What adverse effects are described with inhaled N-acetylcysteine?","choices":["Sedation and confusion","Hypertension and tachycardia","A rotten egg smell, nausea and vomiting","Nosebleed and septal perforation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The smell comes from its sulfur content and is a real reason patients refuse it; the bronchospasm is the one that matters clinically. Bronchospasm. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which adverse effect is characteristic of fibrates?","choices":["Cutaneous flushing","Cholelithiasis","Vitamin malabsorption","Hypersensitivity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fibrate adverse effects are gastrointestinal (nausea, abdominal pain, diarrhea), cholelithiasis and myopathy. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 6-year-old is prescribed ciprofloxacin drops. Two days later the parent reports a white deposit on the eye. What is this?","choices":["A corneal ulcer forming","An expected white precipitate","A fungal superinfection","Dried ointment residue"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and specific enough to ciprofloxacin to be worth warning about in advance. Seen in about one in six patients on this drug. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which of these drugs is a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker?","choices":["Diltiazem","Verapamil","Felodipine","Fosinopril"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The -dipine suffix marks a dihydropyridine; felodipine, isradipine and nisoldipine are approved for hypertension. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What should a patient be warned of after a cycloplegic agent is instilled?","choices":["A bitter taste and stinging","Fixed, small pupils and myopia","Photosensitivity and blurred vision","Conjunctival hyperemia with lash growth"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both follow directly from a dilated pupil and a paralyzed ciliary muscle, so they are predictable rather than idiosyncratic. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"A 57-year-old needs treatment for endophthalmitis with an immediate local effect. Which route achieves this by circumventing absorption entirely?","choices":["Subconjunctival injection","Oral therapy","Intravitreal injection or device","Topical drops"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with a potential sustained effect too. Its limitation is retinal toxicity. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which outcome benefit is traditionally credited to beta blockers in acute coronary syndrome?","choices":["Chest pain relief with no mortality change","Lower early and late mortality","Possibly higher mortality in unstable angina","Dissolving the coronary clot"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers are taught to reduce early and late mortality, along with infarct size, heart failure and sudden cardiac death. Current evidence supports the long-term benefit most clearly when ventricular function is reduced, and early intravenous use can raise the risk of shock. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Nystatin is classified as a nonabsorbable antifungal. Why does that matter?","choices":["It acts only where it touches, and not elsewhere","It must be given with food to be absorbed","It accumulates in the liver with repeated use","It requires renal dose adjustment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Being nonabsorbable is both the safety of the drug and its limitation: it is why it is well tolerated and why it cannot treat anything beyond the mucosa. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"How do alteplase, reteplase and tenecteplase compare in their responses and adverse effects?","choices":["They are very similar","Reteplase causes far less bleeding","Tenecteplase carries no bleeding risk","Alteplase alone causes allergic reactions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The three recombinant tissue plasminogen activators produce very similar responses and adverse effects, chiefly bleeding. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A patient on diltiazem needs a statin. Which statin's levels would diltiazem's CYP3A4 inhibition raise?","choices":["Rosuvastatin","Atorvastatin","Pravastatin","Pitavastatin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The statins raised by non-dihydropyridine CYP3A4 inhibition are atorvastatin, lovastatin and simvastatin. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Beyond the organisms it shares with otitis media, what else causes bacterial rhinosinusitis?","choices":["Streptococcus pyogenes","Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Candida","Mycoplasma and Chlamydophila","Anaerobes exclusively"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The extra names are part of why the starting antibiotic is broader than it is for the ear. Staphylococcus aureus and gram negative bacilli. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which of these is an ocular antihistamine available over the counter?","choices":["Ketotifen","Cromolyn","Bromfenac","Loteprednol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is the one marked over the counter in the antihistamine list, which matters because it is what a patient may already have tried before they reach you. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 55-year-old has been using tobramycin drops for five days and now has worsening pain and a corneal defect. What has happened?","choices":["Rebound hyperemia","Corneal ulceration","Ordinary ocular irritation, common to all ophthalmic antibiotics","A white precipitate from the drug"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, along with reactive keratoconjunctivitis. It is one of the few ocular adverse effects specific enough to name a class. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"How does cyclosporine improve chronic dry eye?","choices":["It inhibits interleukin-2 production","It thickens the tear film to prolong contact time","It blocks cyclooxygenase in the lacrimal gland","It occludes the lacrimal drainage system pharmacologically"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Less inflammation in the lacrimal gland means increased tear production, with improved vision and comfort. Its indication is chronic dry eye associated with inflammation — keratoconjunctivitis sicca. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which otic ingredient is specifically not recommended when the tympanic membrane is perforated or tubes are in place?","choices":["Polymyxin B","Ciprofloxacin","Dexamethasone","Ofloxacin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With a route to the middle ear it risks cochlear damage and hearing loss, which makes an intact drum the thing to establish before prescribing it. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"By what mechanism do ocular glucocorticoids suppress the late-phase allergic reaction?","choices":["They block cyclooxygenase only","They inhibit interleukin-2 production","They inhibit phospholipase A2","They stabilize mast cell membranes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cutting off arachidonic acid stops the whole downstream cascade, not just the prostaglandin arm. They also inhibit fibrin and collagen deposition, which is why they reduce scar formation. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What limits ezetimibe's systemic exposure?","choices":["Enterohepatic recirculation","Extensive CYP3A4 metabolism","Rapid renal excretion","It is not absorbed at all"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ezetimibe and its active glucuronide metabolite circulate enterohepatically, returning the drug to its site of action and limiting systemic exposure. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Under the ACC/AHA (American College of Cardiology/American Heart Association) guideline, why are lipids measured at follow-up visits?","choices":["To titrate to an LDL goal","To detect liver disease early","To decide whether to add niacin","To assess adherence"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Lipids are measured at follow-up to assess adherence to treatment, not to reach a specific LDL (low-density lipoprotein) target. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Which adverse effect of the ocular nonsteroidals matters most in a patient already being watched for glaucoma?","choices":["Reduced tear production","Changes in eyelash length","Bradycardia","A rise in intraocular pressure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The drug given for inflammation pushes the very pressure the glaucoma treatment exists to lower, so the two work against each other. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"When is thiocyanate toxicity most likely during nitroprusside therapy?","choices":["The first minutes of infusion","Long infusions or renal failure","Only in slow acetylators","Only after oral dosing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Thiocyanate accumulates with long-term infusions or renal failure, causing weakness, anoxia, tinnitus, muscle spasms and toxic psychosis. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"How do beta blockers directly change myocardial oxygen supply?","choices":["They dilate the coronary arteries","They relieve coronary vasospasm","They have no effect on supply","They dilate areas of fixed stenosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers act on the demand side, lowering heart rate and contractility; they do not dilate the coronary arteries, so other antianginals are needed to raise supply. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Cholestyramine reduces the absorption of which drug?","choices":["Evolocumab","Nitroprusside","Esmolol","Warfarin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. As anion exchange resins they interfere with the absorption of digoxin, warfarin, thyroxine, beta blockers and thiazide diuretics. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A patient on enalapril has a bothersome dry cough but well-controlled blood pressure. Which change is most appropriate?","choices":["Switch to a different ACE inhibitor","Halve the dose of enalapril","Switch to an angiotensin receptor blocker","Add a beta blocker to suppress it"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Angiotensin receptor blockers do not affect bradykinin metabolism, so they do not cause cough, and they keep the benefit of blocking angiotensin II. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A patient with peripheral vascular disease starts a beta blocker. Which symptom may worsen?","choices":["Leg pain with walking","Ankle swelling","Facial flushing","Constipation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Claudication is listed as a possible beta blocker adverse reaction, which is why peripheral vascular disease is a precaution. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which agent is named as an over-the-counter sleep aid?","choices":["Azelastine","Promethazine","Doxylamine","Fluticasone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A sedating antihistamine sold for the side effect rather than the indication. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which patient falls into one of the four major statin benefit groups?","choices":["A 35-year-old with diabetes and LDL of 120","A 50-year-old with LDL of 150 and 3% risk","A 55-year-old after a myocardial infarction","A 60-year-old with diabetes and LDL of 60"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Clinical atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease is the first benefit group; the others are LDL above 190 mg/dL, diabetes at 40 to 75 with LDL 70 to 189, and high estimated risk. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which of these drugs is a PCSK9 inhibitor?","choices":["Ezetimibe","Evolocumab","Colesevelam","Fenofibrate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Alirocumab and evolocumab are the PCSK9 inhibitors; the -mab suffix marks them as monoclonal antibodies. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Gentamicin and tobramycin act on the bacterial ribosome. At which subunit?","choices":["The 50S subunit","Both the 30S and 50S subunits equally","Neither — they act on the cell membrane","The 30S subunit"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The aminoglycosides bind the 30S subunit and interfere with protein synthesis. The macrolides take the 50S, and keeping the two numbers straight is the whole distinction. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with known angina develops chest pressure at rest. He sits down and places one nitroglycerin tablet under his tongue. Five minutes later the pain is unchanged. What should he do next?","choices":["Wait an hour to see if rest helps","Swallow a long-acting nitrate tablet","Take his beta blocker early","Call emergency services"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chest pain unrelieved five minutes after the first nitroglycerin dose is treated as a possible acute coronary syndrome, so emergency help is called. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A patient starting niacin asks how to reduce the flushing. What is recommended?","choices":["Take it with alcohol","Take it with grapefruit juice","Take aspirin beforehand","Take it with an antacid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Flushing is prostaglandin mediated and is minimized by premedication with aspirin. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"How do fibrates raise HDL (high-density lipoprotein)?","choices":["They block HDL breakdown in the gut","They inhibit HMG-CoA reductase","They bind cholesterol in the bile","They increase ApoA-1 expression"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. PPAR-alpha activation increases expression of ApoA-1, the HDL apolipoprotein, raising HDL by 10 to 35%. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which allergy history is a contraindication to benzonatate?","choices":["Allergy to penicillin or a cephalosporin","Allergy to aspirin or other salicylates","Allergy to iodine or contrast media","Allergy to tetracaine or related anesthetics"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Benzonatate is chemically related to the ester local anesthetics, so a reaction to one predicts a reaction to the other. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"How do dihydropyridines differ from non-dihydropyridines with respect to CYP3A4?","choices":["Substrates, not inhibitors","Stronger CYP3A4 inhibitors","Inducers of CYP3A4","Unaffected by CYP3A4"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. CYP3A4 inhibitors lengthen the half-life of both groups, but only the non-dihydropyridines inhibit CYP3A4 and alter other drugs through it. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Where does ezetimibe act?","choices":["The terminal ileum","The intestinal brush border","The hepatocyte enzyme","Adipose tissue"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ezetimibe selectively inhibits cholesterol absorption at the brush border membrane, blocking sterol uptake in the upper small intestine. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 68-year-old on a prostaglandin analog still has inadequate pressure control. What is the principle for adding a second agent?","choices":["Add a second prostaglandin analog","Increase the prostaglandin to twice daily","Add an agent with a DIFFERENT mechanism","Add a topical steroid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Targeting a second route gives synergy, and fewer drops improves compliance. Which brand pairs which two drugs matters far less than the principle. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"A patient on warfarin is started on gemfibrozil. Which interaction is expected?","choices":["Reduced warfarin absorption","Reduced anticoagulant effect","Increased anticoagulant effect","No interaction is expected"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fibrates increase the anticoagulant effect of warfarin; they also interact with statins, ezetimibe and bile acid sequestrants. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which drug is given by intravenous infusion for hypertensive crisis?","choices":["Hydralazine","Nitroprusside","Minoxidil","Clonidine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nitroprusside is the hypertensive crisis agent, given by intravenous infusion; it dilates both veins and arterioles. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"A contact lens wearer has bacterial conjunctivitis and keratitis has been ruled out. Which class is preferred, and why?","choices":["A macrolide, because erythromycin is soothing on an inflamed eye","An aminoglycoside, because it covers gram-negative organisms","Sulfacetamide, because it is inexpensive and well tolerated","A fluoroquinolone, for Pseudomonas cover"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Contact lens use raises the risk of Pseudomonas aeruginosa, a gram-negative rod, and the fluoroquinolones are the class that covers it. They are also the preferred choice for corneal ulcers for the same reason. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A patient with mild asthma needs a beta blocker after a myocardial infarction. Which choice carries the lowest risk of bronchospasm?","choices":["Metoprolol","Propranolol","Nadolol","Timolol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bronchospasm is less likely with beta-1 selective agents such as metoprolol, because pulmonary beta-2 receptors are relatively spared. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"A patient with hypertension mildly above goal has a urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio of 450 mg/g. Which initial therapy is recommended?","choices":["A dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker","A beta blocker or an alpha-1 blocker","Hydralazine or minoxidil","An ACE inhibitor or receptor blocker"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. With an albumin-to-creatinine ratio of 300 mg/g or more, the algorithm starts an ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor or an angiotensin receptor blocker, consistent with their kidney-protective effect. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"What mediates the cutaneous flushing caused by niacin?","choices":["Prostaglandins","Bradykinin","Histamine","Serotonin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Niacin flushing is a prostaglandin-mediated effect, which is why premedication with aspirin minimizes it. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A patient using an inhaled steroid develops oral candidiasis. Which of these situations is NOT given as a setting for it?","choices":["Use of an inhaled steroid","Human immunodeficiency virus infection","Chemotherapy","Recent tympanostomy tube placement"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct -- this is not one of them. The settings given are inhaled steroids, HIV infection and chemotherapy, all of which share suppressed local or general immunity. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Histamine release produces the triple response. What are its three components?","choices":["Redness, itching and sneezing","Swelling, pallor and numbness","Bronchospasm, hypotension and rash","Redness, a wheal, and a surrounding flare"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Three visible signs from three different mechanisms -- direct vasodilation, leaking capillaries, and a nerve reflex -- which is why they appear together but look different. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What is a patient starting guaifenesin told to expect?","choices":["Less drainage within a day","A bitter taste","More drainage","Drowsiness"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Warning the patient that drainage increases stops them reading the drug working as the drug failing. To drink plenty of fluid. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which three agents are listed as mast cell stabilizers?","choices":["Olopatadine, epinastine and azelastine","Cromolyn, lodoxamide and nedocromil","Ketorolac, nepafenac and diclofenac","Naphazoline, tetrahydrozoline and pheniramine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A short list worth holding because everything else in the allergy section is an antihistamine, a vasoconstrictor or an anti-inflammatory. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which class lowers LDL (low-density lipoprotein) cholesterol the most?","choices":["Statins","Fibrates","Niacin","Ezetimibe"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Statins lower LDL by 18 to 55%, the most of any class; they are the most efficacious and first line. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"What are nasal corticosteroids indicated for?","choices":["Acute bacterial sinusitis","Allergic and vasomotor rhinitis","Otitis externa","Chronic cough"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The same two conditions the nasal antihistamine covers, which is why the two are often used together. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"How does clonidine lower blood pressure?","choices":["Blocks central alpha-2 receptors","Blocks vascular alpha-1 receptors","Blocks renal beta-1 receptors","Stimulates central alpha-2 receptors"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Clonidine is an alpha-2 and imidazoline agonist; stimulating central alpha-2A receptors inhibits sympathetic outflow and increases parasympathetic activity. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"A patient with salt-sensitive hypertension responds poorly to losartan. What improves the response?","choices":["Doubling the losartan dose","Adding an ACE inhibitor","Adding a diuretic","Adding a potassium supplement"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Angiotensin receptor blockers are less effective in the salt-sensitive form; adding a diuretic improves outcomes, which is why most come as combination products. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 28"}]};
